            <ArticleTitle>Treating stridor with opioids: a challenging case of paradoxical vocal cord movement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>130-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2009.02.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 61 year-old patient with a history of anxiety disorder presented with stridor after an uneventful laparotomy with a general anesthetic. Postoperative analgesia was withheld secondary to intermittent oxygen desaturation. She was unresponsive to standard therapies, including racemic epinephrine and albuterol nebulizers. An otolaryngology consultant performed fiberoptic laryngoscopy and paradoxical vocal cord movement was diagnosed. When fentanyl was subsequently administered to treat her pain, the stridor resolved.</AbstractText>
